1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 8: HF beam with traps and ground

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:20,029
YA-NEC course, lesson 8: an HF beam with traps, over real ground. Calculators, Multiband Yagi.

3
00:00:20,079 --> 00:00:28,829
Each band has its own driven dipole and one reflector or director; the dipoles sit on parallel rods, all on one feed.

4
00:00:28,879 --> 00:00:37,007
'Proposal: 6 bands from 40/20/15/10 m'.

5
00:00:37,057 --> 00:00:49,126
Four dipoles - 40, 20, 15, 10 m - plus two traps: on the 15 m dipole for 30 m, on the 10 m dipole for 17 m. Six bands from four rods.

6
00:00:49,176 --> 00:00:56,714
40, 20, 15 and 10 m also get a reflector; 30 and 17 m work as trapped dipoles only.

7
00:00:56,764 --> 00:01:05,336
Average ground (Sommerfeld) at 10 m: real ground, not free space.

8
00:01:05,386 --> 00:01:18,610
'Tune and verify': all lengths, the trap tails and the reflector spacings are tuned together.

9
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:28,074
With 27 wires and 207 segments over Sommerfeld ground this takes almost five minutes - we skip ahead.

10
00:01:28,124 --> 00:01:43,932
Results per band. Centre SWR between 1.13 and 1.30 on all six bands: 40 m 9.2 dBi, 20 m 11.4 dBi, 15 m 6.9, 10 m 8.7 dBi.

11
00:01:43,982 --> 00:01:56,145
F/B: 15 dB on 20 m, 14 on 15 m, 18 on 10 m, but only 8.4 dB on 40 m and almost nothing on the trapped 30 and 17 m.

12
00:01:56,195 --> 00:02:07,042
The band edges tell the truth: on 15 m SWR 7 and 3.9, on 10 m up to 27. Too many elements interact in a small space.

13
00:02:07,092 --> 00:02:17,903
The 'steals' column shows why: on 17 m the 10 m reflector carries 180 % of the current, on 30 m the 15 m reflector 137 %.

14
00:02:17,953 --> 00:02:30,267
'Open as model' puts the whole beam in the 3D structure, ready for the Optimizer.

15
00:02:30,317 --> 00:02:37,467
Six bands on one boom are possible, but the simulator shows the compromises before you build.

16
00:02:37,517 --> 00:02:49,470
Traps and real ground: the multiband beam with all its compromises. Next lesson: other beams - HB9CV, Quad, Hexbeam, LPDA. 73 de IC8TEM
